<h2>One courtroom for the whole family dispute</h2>
<p>Section 26 reflects a sensible economy: the judge already seized of the spouses&rsquo; dispute is best placed to arrange the children&rsquo;s interim life. The provision empowers the court to pass interim orders &ldquo;from time to time&rdquo;, to embed arrangements in the decree, and — critically — to revisit them afterwards on application, by revoking, suspending or varying earlier orders. In Family Court districts such as Delhi, the application is moved in the pending HMA petition itself, typically supported by affidavits on schooling, residence and finances.</p>

<h2>The three heads, in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody and parenting time.</strong> Interim custody with one parent with defined visitation for the other remains the default architecture; courts increasingly craft detailed schedules — weekends, vacations, video calls — rather than vague &ldquo;liberal visitation&rdquo; phrases that breed contempt applications.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Maintenance for the child.</strong> Distinct from spousal maintenance under Sections 24-25: the child&rsquo;s routine expenses, calibrated to the family&rsquo;s standard of living, can be ordered under Section 26 even where the spouse&rsquo;s own claim is contested.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Education.</strong> School and tuition fees, admission costs, and decisions about change of school. Courts resist unilateral mid-litigation school changes by the custodial parent, and routinely direct fee-sharing in proportion to incomes.</div>
</div>

<h2>Interplay with other statutes</h2>
<p>Section 26 does not occupy the field alone. The Guardians and Wards Act, 1890 remains the comprehensive custody code, and Section 21 of the DV Act allows temporary custody orders within a domestic violence proceeding. Child maintenance can equally travel under Section 144 BNSS or Section 20 HAMA. The practical rule for counsel is anti-duplication: pick the forum already seized of the family&rsquo;s dispute, disclose every parallel proceeding, and expect courts to align amounts and arrangements across orders rather than let parents forum-shop between them.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Forum</th><th>When it fits</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 26 HMA, in the divorce petition</td><td>A matrimonial case is pending or a decree exists; interim or post-decree children&rsquo;s arrangements needed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Guardians &amp; Wards Act petition</td><td>No matrimonial proceeding, or long-term guardianship questions beyond the marriage case</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 21 DV Act</td><td>Custody incidental to a protection proceeding by an aggrieved woman</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 144 BNSS / Section 20 HAMA</td><td>Pure child-maintenance claims, especially where speed or a charge on property matters</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Running a Section 26 application well</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Lead with the child&rsquo;s calendar, not the parents&rsquo; grievances.</strong> School timings, existing routines, proximity of each home — the welfare inquiry is logistical before it is moral.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Document the money.</strong> Fee receipts, extracurricular costs, medical needs; ask for direct-payment mechanisms (fees paid to the school) where trust has broken down.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Invoke the sixty-day proviso.</strong> Put the service date on record and press for disposal within the statutory window.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Build in review.</strong> Since Section 26 orders are variable by design, propose a review date — start of the academic year is natural — instead of litigating every change afresh.</div>
</div>
